-- title:
--   A single functional detecting a compact set from an approximate fixed point
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. Let $(K,T)$ be normalized minimal data.
--
--   1. **Uniform diametral bound.** For $a\in K$ and $\eta>0$ there is $\delta>0$ such that $\|Ty-y\|\le\delta$ implies $\|y-a\|\ge1-\eta$.
--   2. **Compact detector.** For a nonempty norm-compact $E\subseteq K$ and $\varepsilon>0$ there is $\delta>0$ with the following property. Every $y\in K$ with $\|Ty-y\|\le\delta$ admits $f\in X^*$, $\|f\|\le1$, with
--   $$
--   f(y-z)\ge1-\varepsilon\qquad\text{for all }z\in E .
--   $$
--   It is obtained from a finite net of $E$, the norming functional of $y$ minus the net's barycenter, and the uniform bound.
